  _ = `Native Support`,
  _ = `Action Sets`,
  _ = `Many games have different control schemes for different parts of the game. You might have one control scheme for walking around on foot, another for driving a vehicle, and yet another when viewing a fullscreen map of the game world.`,
  _ = `Games that support controllers will usually handle switching between these control schemes for you. But if you're playing a game that wasn't built with controllers in mind, or one that doesn't support controller customization to the level you'd like, you can use <1>Action Sets</1> to do it yourself.`,
  _ = `Many games utilize unique control schemes during different scenarios within a game. Within Steam Input we call these Action Sets. For games built without controller support in mind, you may create your own custom Action Sets and switch between them in-game.`,
  _ = `Changing which set is active is a command you can assign to any physical input.`,
  _ = `Action Sets may also be <1>Layers</1>, which are smaller layouts layered over the top of the Action Set, often only changing the way a single physical input is translated.`,
  _ = `For example, some games automatically lower your sensitivity when you zoom in to aim a weapon. For games that don't do it, you could use a Layer to reproduce the behavior.`,
  _ = `First, create a Layer named 'While Aiming', and set it to lower sensitivity. Then add these commands to the Left Trigger:`,
  _ = `With this setup, whenever you pulled the Left Trigger to aim, your sensitivity would automatically be lowered by the While Aiming Layer.`,
  _ = `On Foot`,
  _ = `Shoot`,
  _ = `While Driving`,
